  "content": {
    "id": "p24-q16-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "16",
    "prompt": "Water is flowing down a river at a speed of $2\\text{ m/s}$. Ms Bell rows down the river 96 metres and then returns 96 metres up the river. It takes her 4 seconds longer to row up the river than down the river. How fast does Ms Bell row in still water?",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$10\\text{ m/s}$.\n\nUse upstream speed $v-2$ and downstream speed $v+2$, with time equal to distance divided by speed.",
      "worked": "Let $v\\text{ m/s}$ be her speed in still water, where $v>2$. Her downstream speed is $(v+2)\\text{ m/s}$ and her upstream speed is $(v-2)\\text{ m/s}$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{96}{v-2}-\\frac{96}{v+2}&=4\\\\96(v+2)-96(v-2)&=4(v-2)(v+2)\\\\384&=4(v^2-4)\\\\96&=v^2-4\\\\v^2&=100\\\\v&=10\\end{align*}$$\n\nHer speed in still water is $10\\text{ m/s}$. Check: upstream time $=\\frac{96}{8}=12$ seconds and downstream time $=\\frac{96}{12}=8$ seconds, a difference of 4 seconds.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 84
  },
